# 2026-07-30, X-16(b): main.zig +6, RAISED DELIBERATELY. Same change in all four daemons.
#
#   Every fatal startup failure used to be invisible. `daemonize` points stderr at
#   /dev/null, so the error Zig's start code prints for an error returned from `main`
#   goes to a descriptor nobody reads, and syslog -- the only channel still open after
#   the fork -- never received it. Measured: a daemon told to bind an unusable address
#   exited 0, left nothing listening, and logged "starting" as its last word.
#
#   The fix is the SHAPE of `main`, not a line inside it. The body moved to `runDaemon`
#   and `main` became one line:
#
#       runDaemon() catch |e| return bootstrap_mod.fatal(e);
#
#   so every error converges on one report -- including from a `try` added later.
#   Per-site catch blocks were the alternative, and are how X-7 came to be fixed in one
#   of four copies.
#
#   +6 is the entire cost: three lines of `main`, one blank, and a two-line comment
#   pointing at `bootstrap.fatal` where the reasoning is written once. It was +10 until
#   the gate objected to the same six-line rationale appearing in four files, which was
#   a fair objection and is why it now lives in the library.
#
#   NOT PAID DOWN HERE ON PURPOSE. These four main.zig files are already the roadmap's
#   next extraction target; +6 against a planned several-hundred-line reduction is
#   noise, and this ceiling should be overtaken by that work rather than by a separate
#   tidy-up.

# 2026-07-30, X-16(a): main.zig +3, RAISED DELIBERATELY. Same change in all four daemons.
#
#   The readiness handshake. `daemonize` now returns the write end of a pipe and the
#   ORIGINAL process blocks on the read end instead of exiting 0 immediately, so
#   `service start` finally reports what happened. Measured before: an unbindable
#   listener gave parent exit 0, nothing listening, no process, and rc.d announcing a
#   started service. After: exit 1.
#
#   The daemon has to say when it is actually serving, and only the daemon knows --
#   everything that makes it useful (binding, the worker pool) happens AFTER
#   bootstrap.run returns. So one line lands here, after the pool spawns:
#
#       boot.notifyReady();
#
#   +3 is comment, call, blank. The reasoning is in Bootstrap.notifyReady's doc
#   comment, not repeated here -- the first attempt carried a four-line rationale in
#   all four daemons and the gate objected, correctly, for the second time in one day.
#
#   NOT PAID DOWN HERE ON PURPOSE, same as the X-16(b) note above: these files are the
#   roadmap's settings.zig extraction target and should be cut there, not nibbled here.

# 2026-07-31, D-23: chain.zig 604 -> 606, main.zig 623 -> 626, sealbuild.zig 408 -> 412.
#
#   +9 across three files so an inbound AMS can be validated under the `c=` its
#   sender chose. A milter is handed a header name and value, never the field's
#   original octets, so the separator was fabricated as `name + ": " + value`;
#   `c=simple` hashes the field verbatim, and an AMS whose field carried no space
#   after the colon could not verify. See `securemilter-lib` `header.zig` for the
#   measurement and the rule.
#
#   Where it went: `arc.Header.had_space` and `ArcSet.ams_had_space` (the AMS is
#   part of its own signing input, so its own separator is hashed too), plus the
#   `protocol_flags` entry and short comments. `sealbuild.zig` is the odd one --
#   its four lines buy nothing today, because that path is hardcoded `.relaxed`
#   and relaxed deletes whitespace around the colon either way. It renders through
#   the shared helper regardless, so a future `c=` reaching that path does not
#   silently inherit the bug, and the comment says exactly that.
